# TT.TICKET_INTERFACE -- docs pages, todo list and request classification:
# add_docs_page (S-TICKET-040: sequence pull, "Hot issue" prefix, swallow-all handler),
# remove_docs_page (S-TICKET-041: NO_DATA_FOUND arm is a silent no-op), add_todo_item
# (S-TICKET-049: steps_total/steps_completed only for public items, no history event),
# update_todo_item_status (S-TICKET-050: public/private text, steps_completed adjustments,
# three STRICT sites propagate), clear_todo_list (S-TICKET-048: unconditional delete; the
# ticket lookup only when p_add_history_event = 1), classify_request (S-TICKET-047:
# COUNT-then-UPDATE-or-INSERT, invalid/NULL classification defaults to 2 "standard").
#
# KNOWN DIFFERENCE (known.diff, live 2026-10-21): step 12 passes a bad new status id to
# update_todo_item_status. The routine first UPDATEs the todo item (ticket 954112, seq 0)
# to 999 and only then fails at site #72 (S-TICKET-050, no handler: P0002 on both engines,
# the steps probe agrees). What the CALLER's exception handler leaves behind differs by
# engine, not by translation: PL/SQL keeps the callee's earlier DML in the transaction
# (Oracle probe: todo_item_status_id = 999), whereas a PL/pgSQL EXCEPTION block runs its
# body in a subtransaction and the aborted CALL's writes are undone (PostgreSQL probe: 1).
# A JDBC client catching the error sees the same asymmetry. No translation can reproduce
# Oracle's behaviour here; the difference is recorded so it stays visible (STALE-KNOWN if
# it ever disappears). The engine's other partial-write case, step 13, fails at site #70
# BEFORE any write, so both sides agree there.
#
# ORA_USER=system: the fixtures insert into stop.company, stop.contact, mss.host,
# mss.service and mss.host2service; TT holds only SELECT on those. Rolled back at the end.
ORA_USER=system
COMMITS=no
COMPARE_MSG=no
DESCRIPTION=add/remove_docs_page, add_todo_item, update_todo_item_status, clear_todo_list, classify_request on tickets 954110-954113
